findings.body:
The base size is now read with `malloc_usable_size((void *)o)`, not the `zmalloc_size()` wrapper that the PR description names and the rest of this function uses. This is wrong in three places:

- **Default Linux builds use jemalloc.** `src/Makefile` sets `MALLOC=jemalloc` on Linux, and jemalloc is built with `--with-jemalloc-prefix=je_` (`deps/Makefile`). `zmalloc_size()` expands to `je_malloc_usable_size()` there. The plain `malloc_usable_size()` call resolves to glibc, which then reads a chunk header that jemalloc never wrote. The result is undefined behavior: a garbage size, or a crash.
- **macOS builds break.** `<malloc/malloc.h>` provides `malloc_size()`, not `malloc_usable_size()`, so this is an undeclared function.
- **Non-GNU libc builds.** `zmalloc.c` adds a size prefix to each allocation when `HAVE_MALLOC_SIZE` is not defined, so the plain call would read the wrong address.

The fix is to call the wrapper, which picks the right allocator per platform.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1210-1218"}

::callout{type="security"}
This is undefined behavior on the default allocator, and it can read a bogus size from a field outside the allocation. It is not only a style issue.

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1217-1217"}
    size_t asize = zmalloc_size((void *)o);

The same pattern appears elsewhere in this file as `zmalloc_size(o->ptr)`, so the wrapper is the convention to follow.

The quicklist branch changed from `elesize/elecount*ql->count` to `elesize/samples*ql->count`. `elesize` sums the cost of each sampled node, including `sizeof(quicklistNode)` and the entry allocation, and `elecount` holds the number of entries in those nodes. `ql->count` is the total entry count. The original formula gives the average cost per entry times the total number of entries. The new formula gives the average cost per node times the total number of entries, which multiplies the per-node cost by the entries-per-node factor.

For example, a list of 1000 small entries in 10 nodes of 100 entries each is about 20 KB. The original gives roughly 20 KB. The new formula gives about 2 MB, a 100x overestimate.

This PR does not describe the change and the new test does not cover lists.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1229-1240"}

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1239-1239"}
            if (elecount) asize += (double)elesize/elecount*ql->count;

The `if (elecount)` guard covers an empty sample, which the original code also did not check.

The stream branch changed `asize += lpsize * (s->rax->numele-1);` to `asize += lpsize * s->rax->numele;`. The comment just above the block says the estimate covers the first N-1 listpacks with the sampled average and adds the real size of the last node separately. The code below still does that: it seeks to the last node with `raxSeek(&ri,"$",...)` and adds `zmalloc_size(ri.data)`. With `numele` instead of `numele-1`, the last listpack is counted once in the average and again as its real size.

The overcount is roughly one average listpack per stream. For a stream with a few large listpacks, that can be a large fraction of the reported size.

::diff{file="src/object.c" lines="1318-1334"}

::suggestion{file="src/object.c" lines="1330-1330"}
            asize += lpsize * (s->rax->numele-1);
